* 1.6.1 - Uses chain-gang for signing
* 2.0.0 - First Public release
* 2.1.0 - Renamed to financing-service, configurable UTXO refresh period, CI build and test
* 2.2.0 - Secrets resolved from environment, per-client and admin API keys, per-IP rate limiting, concurrent funding, system requirements document
* 3.0.0 - Breaking: HTTP/2 no longer served (actix-web http2 feature removed to drop the h2 0.3 crate flagged by RUSTSEC-2026-0258) and SR-NFR-006 withdrawn. Also: optional OpenTelemetry trace export via OTLP, dependency updates including OpenTelemetry 0.32 and chain-gang 0.10.1 from crates.io, telemetry startup fix, rotated test key
* 4.0.0 - Breaking: error responses now use a status mapped to their kind (400, 404, 409, 500, 502, 503) instead of 422 for everything; partial_broadcast alone keeps 422. Also: machine-readable error codes on every error response, optional idempotency_key on POST /fund, a locking script per outpoint, each outpoint's value and script reported in the fund response, and the interface/network matrix documented
* 4.1.0 - regtest support: a new `rpc` blockchain interface talking directly to a node, the only one that reaches regtest, with each client's funding address imported into the node's wallet automatically at startup and when a client is added at runtime; chain-gang 0.11.1
* 4.2.0 - optional mapi-lite transaction broadcaster: with a `[mapi_lite]` section funding transactions are submitted to a mapi-lite server instead of the blockchain interface, and `GET /status` reports the broadcaster in use. Also: a new `GET /ready` readiness endpoint, which probes mapi-lite and answers 503 when it is unreachable, leaving `GET /health` a liveness check that no upstream can fail so an outage cannot restart the container; two new `POST /fund` error codes, `broadcast_outcome_unknown` (504) when a submit's fate cannot be determined, whose inputs are then held back rather than offered to the next funding request, and `broadcast_rejected` (409) when the upstream refuses a transaction it will not accept on retry; chain-gang 0.11.2 (bounded allocation when parsing transactions)
* 4.3.0 - Funding correctness, and the fee rate made configurable. **Read `index` in the `POST /fund` response rather than assuming 1:** a funding transaction whose change would be dust no longer carries a change output, so its funded outputs start at 0, and funding the reported maximum always takes that form. Fees are no longer hardcoded: a `[fees]` section sets `satoshis_per_kb`, defaulting to 100, where the superseded expression charged 750 for an ordinary transaction -- so an upgrade without that section pays less, and picks different UTXOs, because a cheaper transaction can be funded by a smaller input. With `[mapi_lite]` configured the rate comes from its fee quote instead, falling back to the configured number when one cannot be had. `GET /balance` reports `max_fundable`, the largest amount a single `POST /fund` can request, and a refusal now distinguishes `insufficient_balance` from `no_suitable_utxo` -- the wallet not holding enough, against holding enough in a shape it cannot spend -- each quoting a figure the caller can act on rather than a single-input estimate that read as "1230 available, 1230 required". The reported balance is derived from the unspent set rather than queried separately, so it cannot disagree with what is spendable, and unconfirmed outputs are eligible for funding. An outpoint the service has spent is held back until the chain reports it, and the change it created held in the cache until then, so a refresh during the gap can no longer hand the same outpoint to the next request; a rejection naming a conflicting transaction is treated as final and reported as `broadcast_rejected` whatever the upstream's retryable flag says, because a conflict is in the inputs and resubmitting cannot help. Cached chain state is reused rather than refetched while it is younger than `service.chain_state_max_age_seconds`, and a refused or uncertain broadcast marks it stale. Outbound requests are bounded by `blockchain_interface.max_requests_per_second`, 3 by default for `woc`; for `woc` that bounds HTTP requests, applied inside chain-gang, because one interface call became many requests when paging arrived. chain-gang 0.11.4: WhatsOnChain UTXO reads follow every page, where `/unspent` stopped at 1000 entries and returned a silently truncated set -- 1000 of 20960 on a busy mainnet address, understating spendable funds by the same margin -- and an address holding an unconfirmed output can be read at all. Also: CI runners pinned off `ubuntu-latest` and moved deliberately to 26.04
* 4.3.1 - A restart no longer hands out outpoints the service has just spent. The inputs a funding transaction spent, and the change it created, were held back only in memory until the chain reported them, so a restart forgot them; the chain it came back to had usually not seen those transactions yet, and the service rebuilt them byte for byte and gave each outpoint to a second caller. That state is now written to disk after every funding commit and read back at startup, so a restarted service carries on from its own unconfirmed change. **The new file must survive a restart**: it sits beside `dynamic_config.filename` as `dynamic.inflight.json`, so a deployment that already keeps `dynamic.toml` across restarts keeps it too; `service.inflight_state_file` overrides the location. Without it the service behaves as 4.3.0 did. Also chain-gang 0.11.5: WhatsOnChain goes on listing an output after a mempool transaction spends it, flagged `isSpentInMempoolTx`, and earlier releases reported such outputs as unspent; they are now left out, so a transaction still unconfirmed after its ten-minute reservation does not have its input offered again
* 4.4.0 - Funding under concurrent load, and a fee that pays the rate it quotes. **A refusal whose funds are only in flight is now HTTP 503, not 409:** a request arriving while every UTXO it could use is claimed by requests still broadcasting is refused with the new code `funds_in_flight` and `Retry-After: 1`, and its `idempotency_key` is released, so it can be retried unchanged -- it is given only when the change those requests return will cover it. `insufficient_balance` and `no_suitable_utxo` keep their 409 for a wallet that is short however they settle, so a retry policy or alert keyed on status stops treating a busy wallet as an empty one; a client that branched on those codes for that case should handle `funds_in_flight` too. Concurrent requests for one client no longer spend the same input: each claims its inputs while it plans, under the client's lock, and the broadcast runs outside it, where planning under a shared lock let every request pick the same smallest UTXO and all but one be refused as conflicts. Nor are they handed the same outpoint: identical requests built byte-identical transactions, and the upstream answers a transaction it already holds with success, so several callers were each given the one outpoint -- the service now refuses to hand out a transaction twice, and logs it. The fee is priced on the transaction actually built -- each funded output's real size, and the varints that grow with the number of inputs and outputs -- where each funded output was costed 9 bytes short and transactions paid less than `satoshis_per_kb`; an ordinary funding transaction is 226 bytes, 23 satoshi at 100 sat/KB
* 4.5.0 - With `[mapi_lite]`, writes no longer queue on the chain refresh, and the in-flight state is written once per burst of commits rather than once per commit. **With `[mapi_lite]` configured, a funding request whose cached chain state has merely aged past `service.chain_state_max_age_seconds` is funded from it**, and the refresh runs behind the request; it still waits when the cache has never been loaded or a refused or uncertain broadcast has marked it stale, and without `[mapi_lite]` nothing changes. A write can therefore be built on state up to one refresh older than the window. The service applies its own spends as it makes them, so that matters only when something else spends from the same key, and a refused broadcast still makes the next write wait for a fresh read. One refresh per client is in flight at a time, shared by every request that finds the state stale, where a burst used to queue one fetch each behind the WhatsOnChain limiter for the same answer -- capping writes at about 3 a second -- and the answers landed out of order and handed back inputs already spent. Change the service created and then spent is held until the read interface is seen to know of the spend, where an index part way through a run of spends could report it unspent and have it offered again as a double spend. Commits share writes of the in-flight state: each request is still answered only after a write that includes it, but a burst costs one write between them, and the file is compact JSON, written off the request worker. Every funding broadcast is logged before it is sent and again with its outcome, each line naming its txid; a release build keeps only the failure line. chain-gang 0.13.0, with mapi-lite pinned at v0.3.0: 0.11.6 fixed signing for about one client key in 345, every one of whose funding transactions had been refused on chain as NULLFAIL; 0.12 and 0.13 change nothing the service uses
* 5.0.0 - Breaking: settings load as Event's and mapi-lite's do (CS-624), so a configuration file or deployment from 4.x needs changing before it starts. `FS_CONFIG` names the TOML file, and no longer holds JSON; any setting can be overridden with `FS__<TABLE>__<KEY>`, and the named overrides `FS_ADMIN_API_KEY`, `FS_RPC_USER`, `FS_RPC_PASSWORD`, `FS_MAPI_LITE_AUTH_TOKEN`, `FS_CLIENT_<ID>_WIF` and `FS_CLIENT_<ID>_API_KEY` are no longer read. A key no setting reads stops startup, naming it, where it used to be ignored. `blockchain_interface.network_type` is now `network`, spelt `mainnet`, `testnet`, `regtest` or `stn`. A top-level `listen_addr` replaces `[web_interface] address` and `port` and the `APP_ENV` switch; `RUST_LOG` replaces `[logging]`; `mapi_lite.auth_token` is the token alone, without `Bearer `; and `use_mapi_fee_quote` and `mapi_lite`'s `timeout_seconds`, `max_retries` and `health_timeout_seconds` are refused, the timings now derived from `total_timeout_seconds` and the submit ceiling mapi-lite publishes. **On mainnet and testnet** an admin API key and an `api_key` for every client are required, `POST /client` takes a key only by `wif_env` (CS-514), and with `[mapi_lite]` its `miner_id_public_keys` are required (CS-624). Startup also refuses a mapi-lite whose nodes are on another network than `network` (CS-624), a mapi-lite token mapi-lite refuses (CS-632), and a client key for the other network (CS-634). A runtime client's `wif_env` must start with `dynamic_config.wif_env_prefix`, `FS_RUNTIME_WIF_` by default, and the dynamic config file holds `env:` references only, written atomically and owner-only (CS-526). Also: `GET /ready` reports chain-read health as well as mapi-lite's (CS-505). **Without `[mapi_lite]` it is no longer always 200:** it answers 503, with a `chain` check naming the client and the age, once any client's last successful chain read is older than the new `service.ready_max_chain_read_age_seconds` -- three `utxo_refresh_period`s by default, never under 60s, `0` turns it off -- or, with no clients, once the blockchain interface last answered longer ago than that. With `[mapi_lite]` a funding request goes on from cached state without waiting for a refresh, so failing chain reads used to go unnoticed while the cache aged. The mapi-lite check asks mapi-lite's own `GET /ready` rather than its fee quote, which mapi-lite serves from its configuration and answered with every node down. An unreachable blockchain interface at startup is a warning rather than an exit: a single WhatsOnChain 429 used to stop the service, and the restart met the same rate limit; it now starts, `/health` and `/status` serve, and `/ready` answers 503 until the first chain read succeeds. apigun's `LOCKING_SCRIPT` is required and has no default, so a load test can no longer pay real coins to a script the caller never chose (#107). Metrics over OTLP (CS-608), off by default and turned on by `OTEL_METRICS_EXPORTER=otlp` or `[telemetry] enabled = true`: `http_requests`, `http_request_duration`, `ready`, `fs_fund_requests`, `fs_client_balance` and `fs_client_chain_state_age`, documented with suggested alerts under Metrics in Configuration.md. Chain state can be read through mapi-lite with `interface_type = "mapi_lite"` (CS-601), which the sample config now uses so mapi-lite is the deployment's only WhatsOnChain client; `woc` alongside `[mapi_lite]` on mainnet or testnet is warned about (CS-635). `FS__MAPI_LITE__MINER_ID_PUBLIC_KEYS` takes the keys comma-separated (CS-636). A mapi-lite response is believed only when signed by a listed miner ID, the quoted fee is capped (CS-523), and a mapi-lite signing with an unlisted key is reported not ready and funds nothing (CS-618). A broadcast that may have landed is never reported as nothing spent (CS-501); every blockchain interface call has a deadline (CS-502); uls-client v0.6.1 (CS-627, CS-637). Release builds no longer compile out `info` and `debug` logging (CS-630). The image runs as a non-root user and is built `--locked` from digest-pinned bases (CS-527), and CI publishes it to `ghcr.io/nchain-innovation/financing-service` (CS-606).
* 5.0.1 - Takes rustls-webpki 0.103.16 and webpki-root-certs 1.0.10, for RUSTSEC-2026-0382 (certificate verification may accept distrusted intermediates) and RUSTSEC-2026-0383 (bundled root certificates trust distrusted emSign intermediates). No other change. These advisories were published after 5.0.0 was tagged, and its tag build stopped at `cargo audit`, so 5.0.0 has no published image: use 5.0.1, whose upgrade notes are 5.0.0's.
* 5.1.0 - **An unknown `client_id` is now `401 unauthorized`, not `unknown_client`, once any client has an API key** (CS-661): it is answered exactly as a missing or wrong key is, so whether a client with a key exists cannot be learned without that key, where `GET /client/{id}/balance` answered 401 for a real client and 404 for an unknown one. The id is logged at `info`, so a mistyped `client_id` can still be told from a wrong key on the server. With no client keys at all, an unknown id is `unknown_client` as before; a caller that branched on `unknown_client` to catch a misconfigured id while keys are in use will see `unauthorized` instead. `broadcaster_untrusted` now releases the request's `idempotency_key` and its `[funding_limits]` hold, since nothing was sent (CS-660): once an operator fixes mapi-lite's key, the same request, key and all, is funded, where it was refused `key_in_progress` for the key's TTL and its amount counted against the client's window for the whole period. A `multiple_tx` request that meets an untrusted broadcaster after its first transaction went out is still `partial_broadcast` and keeps its key, so a resend is answered with what happened. The `Bearer` scheme is matched in any case and may be followed by more than one space (CS-663), as RFC 9110 allows; a request carrying both `Authorization: Bearer` and `X-API-Key` is judged on the Bearer key alone, which now includes a lowercase `bearer`. Also: uls-client and uls-core v0.7.0 (CS-641), with no change to this service's use of them; a non-Unix build no longer fails `-D warnings` on an unused import (CS-638). The image is now published to `ghcr.io/teranode-group/financing-service-rs`, and only for a `vX.Y.Z` tag; images up to 5.0.1 stay at `ghcr.io/nchain-innovation/financing-service`.
* Unreleased - Breaking: **`DELETE /client/{client_id}` takes the admin key, not the client's own** (CS-653), as `POST /client` does: a client's key, which every service funded through it holds, could take that client offline, and with it the in-flight reservations from the next save of the in-flight file. And it removes only a client added at runtime: a client from the configuration file is refused with the new code `static_client` (409) and goes on being served, where it was dropped from memory -- its `/fund` and balance answering `unknown_client` -- until a restart brought it back. With an admin key, an unknown id is `unknown_client` (404).
